Automated grading systems can evaluate and provide feedback on students' code based on various metrics. The quality of this feedback is crucial in enhancing students' performance, regardless of the metric used. This study examines how providing feedback on code coverage impacts students' performance, as measured by both the number of lines covered and a grading scale. The investigation was conducted using a randomized trial, with two groups of students receiving feedback on either the percentage of lines covered or the areas of the code covered. The results revealed that receiving feedback on code areas resulted in higher performance grades compared to feedback on percentage alone. Thus, incorporating both types of feedback may be beneficial for instructors seeking to optimize their grading systems. Furthermore, we identified a code coverage threshold beyond which additional feedback no longer improved students' performance, suggesting that the feedback's impact depends on the students' baseline code quality. Finally, we discovered that the benefits resulting from providing feedback on code areas were due, at least partially, to the system used to calculate the percentage of lines covered. When the system's biases were removed, the advantages were no longer observed, implying the need to account for potential grading biases when evaluating the feedback's effect. These findings can be applied to other automated grading systems that involve metrics that reflect a aspect of a student's performance. For example, similar research could be done for feedback on the execution time of a program or other similar metrics. Additionally, future work could be directed at removing biases from the feedback, in addition to exploring its impact, in order to optimize it. In addition, the study's results can be applied to other educational scenarios in which providing feedback on a metric may improve a student's performance, in addition to its potential impact on performance. For examp